  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Plait Solitaire. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Plait Solitaire (also known as 'Plaits' or 'The Braid') is a double-deck solitaire game of significant complexity and visual appeal. It is distinguished by the 'Plait'â€”a central reserve of 13 cards arranged in a braid-like pattern, where only specific cards are accessible at any time. Requiring the player to manage two sets of foundations and eight tableau piles, it is a game of foresight, sequence planning, and careful reserve management.",
    objective: 'Move all 104 cards to 8 foundation piles, building up in suit from the Base rank (wrapping K to A).',
    gameplay: 'Build tableaus down in sequence and alternating color. Only the top card of each tableau pile and the reserve (plait) is available for play on the foundations. One card or a group of cards in proper sequence may move to another tableau pile. When a tableau is empty, any card or legal sequence may fill it. When no moves are available, turn cards from the stock one at a time; cards from stock may move to tableau or foundations. No redeal.',
    keyRules: [
      'Foundations build up in same suit from base rank, wrapping King to Ace.',
      'Tableau builds down in alternating color; only top cards and plait top are available for foundations.',
      'One card or a legal sequence may move to another tableau; empty tableau accepts any card or legal sequence.',
      'Turn stock one card at a time when no other moves; no redeal.',
    ],
